Issues.Body:
`ngtsc` now elides imports that are only referenced from the Angular decorators it removes, or from the `emitDecoratorMetadata` types of the declarations those decorate, matching the output of `ngp` (split out from #72174).

Custom `beforeTs` transformers in `NgtscProgram.emit` run ahead of `importPreservingTransformer` so any runtime references they synthesize are preserved. Local compilation, `.d.ts` output, JSX files, and non-Angular decorator metadata are unchanged.

```ts
import {Component, QueryList, ViewChildren} from '@angular/core';

@Component({selector: 'my-cmp', template: ''})
export class MyCmp {
  @ViewChildren('x') items!: QueryList<unknown>;
}
```

Before (under `emitDecoratorMetadata: true`):

```js
import { Component, QueryList, ViewChildren } from '@angular/core';
import * as i0 from "@angular/core";
// ...
```

After:

```js
import { Component, ViewChildren } from '@angular/core';
import * as i0 from "@angular/core";
// ...
```

### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

core

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description


The boundary shows the fallback when a constructor throws inside `@defer`. But a component created before the throwing sibling keeps its subscription alive, even with `takeUntilDestroyed()`. Each `$reset()` adds another one.

`LiveOrders` subscribes to `OrdersService.updates`. `OrderTotal` then throws because `USDD` is an invalid currency code.

```ts
import { Component, Service, inject, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { takeUntilDestroyed } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';
import { Subject } from 'rxjs';

@Service()
export class OrdersService {
  readonly orderCount = 3;
  readonly total = 100;
  readonly updates = new Subject<void>();
  readonly count = signal(0);
}

@Component({ selector: 'live-orders', template: '<p>Orders: {{ orders.orderCount }}</p>' })
class LiveOrders {
  readonly orders = inject(OrdersService);

  constructor() {
    this.orders.updates
      .pipe(takeUntilDestroyed())
      .subscribe(() => this.orders.count.update((value) => value + 1));
  }
}

@Component({
  selector: 'order-total',
  template: '<p>Total: {{ formatter.format(orders.total) }}</p>',
})
class OrderTotal {
  readonly orders = inject(OrdersService);

  readonly formatter = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
    style: 'currency',
    currency: 'USDD',
  });
}

@Component({
  imports: [LiveOrders, OrderTotal],
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    @boundary {
      @defer (on immediate) {
        <live-orders />
        <order-total />
      }
    } @error {
      <p>Could not load orders.</p>
      <button (click)="$reset()">Retry</button>
    }
    <button (click)="orders.updates.next()">Emit</button>
    <p id="notifications">Notifications: {{ orders.count() }}</p>
  `,
})
export class App {
  readonly orders = inject(OrdersService);
}
```

1. Wait for Retry.
2. Click Retry once and wait for the fallback again.
3. Click Emit once.

I expected `Notifications: 0`. I get `Notifications: 2`: both failed attempts still react to the same emission.


### Anything else?

This also happens without `@defer`. Moving the error to `ngOnInit` cleans up both subscriptions.

In the constructor case, `DestroyRef.onDestroy`, `ngOnDestroy`, and subscription finalization never run for `LiveOrders`. Destroying the fixture afterward doesn't clean them up either.

If a destroy hook throws while the platform is being destroyed, the error escapes the `setTimeout` in `asyncDestroyPlatform()` and ends the process on servers that use `AngularAppEngine`. #34768 added a `try/finally` there, but that still lets the error through.

It doesn't matter whether the component is routed or not. A component that creates a subscription once an HTTP call answers ends the process if the visitor leaves before the answer:

```ts
export class Profile implements OnInit, OnDestroy {
  private readonly http = inject(HttpClient);
  private readonly updates = new Subject<string>();
  private subscription!: Subscription;
  protected readonly name = signal('');

  ngOnInit() {
    this.http.get<{ name: string }>('http://localhost:4400/api/profile').subscribe(({ name }) => {
      this.name.set(name);
      this.subscription = this.updates.subscribe((next) => this.name.set(next));
    });
  }

  ngOnDestroy() {
    // undefined if the visitor left before the answer
    this.subscription.unsubscribe();
  }
}
```

A routed component behind an HTTP resolver only needs a plain subscribe. In a zoneless app, if the request is aborted right as the resolver answers, the component is destroyed before its first change detection and `ngOnDestroy` runs without `ngOnInit`:

```ts
export const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: '',
    component: Dashboard,
    resolve: { user: () => inject(HttpClient).get('http://localhost:4400/api/user') },
  },
];

export class Dashboard implements OnInit, OnDestroy {
  private readonly notifications = inject(Notifications);
  private subscription!: Subscription;
  protected readonly unread = signal(0);

  ngOnInit() {
    this.subscription = this.notifications.unread$.subscribe((n) => this.unread.set(n));
  }

  ngOnDestroy() {
    // can run before ngOnInit
    this.subscription.unsubscribe();
  }
}
```

The error is now caught and logged. angular/angular#72139 also catches it in the `@angular/platform-server` copy. Here it is logged instead of rejected, since nothing awaits the destroy.

Related to #34754

`oxc-transform` previously only elided `ɵsetClassMetadata` / `ɵsetClassMetadataAsync` / `ɵsetClassDebugInfo` calls inside `FunctionExpression` / `ArrowFunctionExpression` IIFEs, and `analyzeClassStaticProperties()` set `shouldWrap = false` whenever a class contained any `StaticBlock` other than `this.<prop> = <expr>`.

When full-mode Angular classes emit metadata inside an ES2022 `static { ... }` block (as `ngp` already does, and as proposed for `ngtsc` in angular/angular#72136 — including `@angular/core/fesm2022/core.mjs`, where Rollup bundles internal `setClassMetadata` calls without the `ɵ` prefix), `oxc-transform` previously left the `static {}` block intact and skipped wrapping the class in a `/*#__PURE__*/` IIFE, preventing unused classes such as `PlatformRef` and `ApplicationModule` from being tree-shaken (+10.0 kB in standalone apps).

This updates `oxc-transform` and `traversal` to:
- Recognize `ɵsetClassMetadata*` calls and `ngDevMode`/`ngJitMode`-guarded `(ɵ)setClassMetadata*` statements inside `StaticBlock`s, removing metadata-only `StaticBlock`s (and individual metadata statements inside multi-statement `StaticBlock`s) when `advancedOptimizations` is enabled.
- Allow `analyzeClassStaticProperties()` to continue wrapping classes whose `StaticBlock`s contain only elidable Angular metadata and/or pure `this.<prop> = <expr>` assignments, while still leaving user side-effectful `StaticBlock`s unwrapped.

### Minimal failure example

**Input (`@angular/core` / `ngp` / `ngtsc` ES2022 output):**
```js
export class PlatformRef {
  static ɵfac = function PlatformRef_Factory(t) { return new (t || PlatformRef)(); };
  static ɵprov = /*@__PURE__*/ ɵɵdefineInjectable({ token: PlatformRef, factory: PlatformRef.ɵfac, providedIn: 'platform' });
  static {
    (typeof ngDevMode === "undefined" || ngDevMode) &&
      setClassMetadata(PlatformRef, [{ type: Injectable, args: [{ providedIn: 'platform' }] }], null, null);
  }
}
```

**Before (`main`):**
Left unchanged (`shouldWrap = false`), preventing `PlatformRef` from being wrapped in `/*#__PURE__*/ (() => { ... })()` and retaining it in standalone application bundles.

**After:**
```js
export let PlatformRef = /*#__PURE__*/ (() => {
  class PlatformRef {
    static ɵfac = function PlatformRef_Factory(t) { return new (t || PlatformRef)(); };
    static ɵprov = /*@__PURE__*/ ɵɵdefineInjectable({ token: PlatformRef, factory: PlatformRef.ɵfac, providedIn: 'platform' });
  }
  return PlatformRef;
})();
```

### Is this a regression?

No. The deferred close was added in #33697.

### Description

`ComboboxPattern.closePopupOnFocusout()` defers closing the popup with a `setTimeout` that is never cancelled:

```ts
closePopupOnFocusout() {
  // Give focus some time to move before we check it.
  setTimeout(() => {
    const comboboxFocused = this.isFocused();
    const popupFocused = !!this.inputs.popup()?.isFocused();

    if (!this.inputs.alwaysExpanded() && !comboboxFocused && !popupFocused) {
      this.inputs.expanded.set(false);
    }
  });
}
```

If the combobox is destroyed between the `focusout` and the timer, for example when a route change removes a combobox whose popup has focus, or when a test fixture is torn down right after a focus move, the timer writes `expanded` after destruction. `expanded` is a `model()`, so the write emits through a destroyed `OutputRef` and Angular logs:

```text
NG0953: Unexpected emit for destroyed `OutputRef`. The owning directive/component is destroyed.
```

Angular drops the emit, so there is no functional effect beyond the warning. The warning is logged in production builds too, with the code only.

### Reproduction

1. Render an `ngCombobox` with a popup (e.g. `ngComboboxWidget` with an `ngListbox`) inside an `@if`.
2. Open the popup and focus an element inside it, or the combobox input.
3. Move focus out (dispatch `focusout`), and in the same task set the `@if` condition to `false` and run change detection, so the combobox is destroyed.
4. Let the next macrotask run.

A `TestBed` sketch:

```ts
fixture.componentInstance.open();                // popup expanded, input focused
input.dispatchEvent(new FocusEvent('focusout', { bubbles: true }));
fixture.componentInstance.show.set(false);       // removes the combobox via @if
fixture.detectChanges();
await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve));
// console.warn: NG0953: Unexpected emit for destroyed `OutputRef` ...
```

Whether it appears depends on timing: if the timer runs before destruction there is no warning.

### Expected behavior

No write to `expanded` after the combobox is destroyed, either by clearing the pending timer when the directive is destroyed, or by checking for destruction before `expanded.set(false)`.

### Environment

- `@angular/aria` 22.2.7; the same code is in 22.3.0, 22.4.0-next.0 and on `main`
- `@angular/core` 22.x, zoneless
- Seen in unit tests (Vitest + jsdom) and in the browser when navigating away with the popup focused
